{"tool_name":"gsea","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"gsea","home":"http://www.broadinstitute.org/gsea/index.jsp","summary":"Computational method that determines whether an a priori defined set of genes shows statistically significant, concordant differences between two biological states (e.g. phenotypes).","addition_date":"2017-01-17T15:08:23Z","last_update_date":"2022-06-20T14:18:17.457292Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Desktop application","Library"],"collections":["ELIXIR Trainer Tools"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:gsea","credit":[{"typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"],"url":"http://software.broadinstitute.org/gsea/contact.jsp"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://software.broadinstitute.org/cancer/software/gsea/wiki/index.php/Main_Page"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["sergitobara"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Differential gene expression analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3223"},{"term":"Gene expression analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2495"},{"term":"Gene expression correlation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3463"}]}],"name":"GSEA","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"training_go_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1073/pnas.0506580102","metadata":{"abstract":"Although genomewide RNA expression analysis has become a routine tool in biomedical research, extracting biological insight from such information remains a major challenge. Here, we describe a powerful analytical method called Gene Set Enrichment Analysis (GSEA) for interpreting gene expression data. The method derives its power by focusing on gene sets, that is, groups of genes that share common biological function, chromosomal location, or regulation. We demonstrate how GSEA yields insights into several cancer-related data sets, including leukemia and lung cancer. Notably, where single-gene analysis finds little similarity between two independent studies of patient survival in lung cancer, GSEA reveals many biological pathways in common. The GSEA method is embodied in a freely available software package, together with an initial database of 1,325 biologically defined gene sets. \u00a9 2005 by The National Academy of Sciences of the USA.","authors":[{"name":"Ebert B.L."},{"name":"Gillette M.A."},{"name":"Golub T.R."},{"name":"Lander E.S."},{"name":"Mesirov J.P."},{"name":"Mootha V.K."},{"name":"Mukherjee S."},{"name":"Paulovich A."},{"name":"Pomeroy S.L."},{"name":"Subramanian A."},{"name":"Tamayo P."}],"citationCount":23096,"date":"2005-10-25T00:00:00Z","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America","title":"Gene set enrichment analysis: A knowledge-based approach for interpreting genome-wide expression profiles"},"pmcid":"PMC1239896","pmid":"16199517"},{"doi":"10.1038/ng1180","metadata":{"abstract":"DNA microarrays can be used to identify gene expression changes characteristic of human disease. This is challenging, however, when relevant differences are subtle at the level of individual genes. We introduce an analytical strategy, Gene Set Enrichment Analysis, designed to detect modest but coordinate changes in the expression of groups of functionally related genes. Using this approach, we identify a set of genes involved in oxidative phosphorylation whose expression is coordinately decreased in human diabetic muscle. Expression of these genes is high at sites of insulin-mediated glucose disposal, activated by PGC-1\u03b1 and correlated with total-body aerobic capacity. Our results associate this gene set with clinically important variation in human metabolism and illustrate the value of pathway relationships in the analysis of genomic profiling experiments.","authors":[{"name":"Altshuler D."},{"name":"Carlsson E."},{"name":"Daly M.J."},{"name":"Eriksson K.-F."},{"name":"Golub T.R."},{"name":"Groop L.C."},{"name":"Hirschhorn J.N."},{"name":"Houstis N."},{"name":"Lander E.S."},{"name":"Laurila E."},{"name":"Lehar J."},{"name":"Lindgren C.M."},{"name":"Mesirov J.P."},{"name":"Mootha V.K."},{"name":"Patterson N."},{"name":"Puigserver P."},{"name":"Ridderstrale M."},{"name":"Sihag S."},{"name":"Spiegelman B."},{"name":"Subramanian A."},{"name":"Tamayo P."}],"citationCount":5554,"date":"2003-07-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nature Genetics","title":"PGC-1\u03b1-responsive genes involved in oxidative phosphorylation are coordinately downregulated in human diabetes"},"pmid":"12808457"}],"topic":[{"term":"Gene expression","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0203"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"GSEA","home":"https://bio.tools/gsea","summary":"Computational method that determines whether an a priori defined set of genes shows statistically significant, concordant differences between two biological states (e.g. phenotypes).","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:gsea/app/www.broadinstitute.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":33876,"citations":[{"count":3,"year":"2005"},{"count":38,"year":"2006"},{"count":77,"year":"-1"},{"count":114,"year":"2007"},{"count":218,"year":"2008"},{"count":321,"year":"2009"},{"count":426,"year":"2010"},{"count":518,"year":"2011"},{"count":608,"year":"2012"},{"count":717,"year":"2013"},{"count":832,"year":"2014"},{"count":999,"year":"2015"},{"count":1168,"year":"2016"},{"count":1364,"year":"2017"},{"count":1437,"year":"2018"},{"count":1858,"year":"2019"},{"count":2292,"year":"2026"},{"count":2577,"year":"2020"},{"count":3572,"year":"2021"},{"count":3580,"year":"2024"},{"count":3591,"year":"2023"},{"count":3651,"year":"2025"},{"count":3915,"year":"2022"}],"doi":"10.1073/pnas.0506580102","pmcid":"PMC1239896","pmid":"16199517","ref_co